# Exclude from the Docker build context (keeps images small & builds fast).
#
# Anchoring is the whole game here, and getting it wrong leaked secrets. The
# build context is the REPO ROOT (deploy/docker-compose.yml sets `context: ..`)
# and deploy/Dockerfile ends with `COPY . /app`, so every pattern below is the
# only thing between a file and the published image.
#
# A pattern without a slash matches ONLY at the context root. So a bare `.env`
# excluded /.env while `COPY . /app` happily baked in deploy/.env — the file
# install.sh writes, and the one .env.example tells owners to put
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY and AVA_PASSWORD into. install.sh always passes --build, so
# that was every USER's key in their own image layer, not just the maintainer's.
# Hence the `**/` forms below.
#
# Same reasoning as .gitignore's deliberately wide globs (.gitignore:12-17,
# :59-63): config_mgmt.update_config snapshots .env -> .env.bak and
# settings._write_config snapshots ava.yaml -> ava.yaml.bak on every Setup save,
# so the .bak and .tmp forms need covering too, not just the live file.
#
# Do NOT widen `data`/`logs`/`media`/`models` to `**/`. They are root-anchored on
# purpose: frontend/src/components/data/ is real tracked source, and `**/data`
# would drop DataView.tsx out of the web build stage.
#
# deploy/profiles/*.env and deploy/default-model.env stay IN: they are committed,
# secret-free, and read on the host by compose/install.sh rather than in-image.

.git
.venv
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc
node_modules
frontend/node_modules
frontend/dist

# --- secrets and owner-local config, at ANY depth -----------------------------
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example
**/secrets
**/*.secret
**/ava.yaml
**/ava.yaml.*
**/.ava.yaml.*
**/*.bak
**/ava-data

# --- runtime trees, ROOT-ANCHORED on purpose (see the note above) -------------
/data
/logs
/media
/models
/enroll
bin/piper
searxng
**/*.log

# --- connectors/: default-deny, allow-list the shipped built-ins --------------
# ava_bridge/connectors.py sets BUILTIN_DIR = <repo root>/connectors, so anything
# in this directory at build time loads as a first-party built-in — no install
# step, no manifest edit, present for every user of the image.
#
# On a maintainer's box AVA_HOME is the repo root, so `connectors/` is also where
# that box's OWN apps get installed at runtime. .gitignore keeps those out of the
# REPO; nothing kept them out of the IMAGE, and `COPY . /app` copies the working
# tree, not the index. install.sh always passes --build, so a private app sitting
# on disk shipped to whoever pulled the image.
#
# Hence the allow-list rather than a list of names to exclude: a new private app
# is excluded the moment it appears, without anyone remembering to add it here
# (and without publishing its name in a tracked file). Adding a genuinely shipped
# built-in is a deliberate `!connectors/<id>` line — tests/test_no_private_apps_
# shipped.py fails if this block stops covering the untracked directories.
connectors/*
!connectors/README.md
!connectors/_template
!connectors/bridge
!connectors/local-llm
!connectors/router

# --- agent/: the same hole, one layer down -----------------------------------
# These two trees are RENDERED from connector manifests, never authored — the
# egress policies and agent tools `ava connector generate` writes. .gitignore
# keeps them out of the repo; nothing kept them out of the IMAGE, and the same
# `COPY . /app`-copies-the-working-tree reasoning above applies verbatim: a
# maintainer's private app shipped its generated tools and its egress policy to
# whoever pulled the image.
#
# They are also runtime state, so they belong under AVA_HOME
# (settings.agent_state_dir()) rather than in the image at all. Excluding them
# keeps the legacy code-root path empty in a fresh container, which is what makes
# settings.migrate_agent_state() a no-op there instead of a resurrection.
agent/mcp_server_connectors/apps/
agent/policies/generated/
